<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Su Song</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1020–1101</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Zirong, 蘇頌</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Su <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> was a Chinese polymathic scientist and statesman of the Song dynasty, working in mathematics, astronomy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartography">cartography</a>, metallurgy, mechanical and hydraulic engineering and poetry. He was the engineer of a hydro-mechanical astronomical clock tower at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armillary-sphere">armillary sphere</a> was the first to use a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mechanical-clock">mechanical clock</a> drive and which had the oldest known endless power-transmitting chain drive. His treatise on the tower, the Xinyi Xiangfayao, was written in 1092 and printed in 1094. As an official he became Minister of Justice in 1086 and went on a diplomatic mission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a> in 1077.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Su <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> was of Hokkien ancestry and was born in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, near medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a>. His junior colleague, the Hanlin scholar Ye Mengde, wrote that in his youth he topped the provincial examination list for the best essay on the principles and structure of the Chinese calendar. An early interest in astronomy and calendrical science led him towards a career as a state official. Like his contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-kuo">Shen Kuo</a>, he was a polymath. In his spare time he wrote poetry and collected old artworks from earlier dynasties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He rose to be Ambassador and President of the Ministry of Personnel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> and was known as an expert in administration and finance. He became Minister of Justice in 1086 and later Vice President of the Chancellery Secretariat. At court he kept clear of the rivalry between the Conservatives under Sima Guang and the Reformists under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-anshi">Wang Anshi</a>, although many of his associates were Conservatives.</p><p>In 1077 he went on a mission to the Khitan Liao dynasty, which had made its own calendar in 994. He was due to arrive for a birthday feast on a day that matched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> winter solstice but fell a day behind the Liao calendar. He admitted to the emperor that the Khitan calendar was slightly more accurate, and officials of the Bureau of Astronomy and Calendar were punished. In 1081 the court had him compile the diplomatic history of Song–Liao relations, which filled 200 volumes, and his knowledge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartography">cartography</a> helped settle a border dispute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In 1086 the emperor ordered Su Song to rebuild the armillary clock for a new tower in the capital. A wooden model was finished in 1088, bronze parts were cast by 1090 and the whole was complete by 1094. The tower stood about 40 feet high and was driven by water. It had a rotating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armillary-sphere">armillary sphere</a>, a bronze celestial globe 4.5 feet across and mannequins that appeared at small doors to announce the hours. His treatise, with 47 detailed illustrations, was written in 1092.</p><p>The tower was later taken apart. His son Su Xie, ordered by Emperor Gaozong to build another, failed, and thought his father had left essential parts out of his diagrams. Derk Bodde notes that the clock led to no mass production, as the work served the imperial court. Su <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> also made five star maps, the oldest surviving printed star maps, and in 1070 led the compilation of the Bencao Tujing, an illustrated pharmacopoeia covering plants, animals and minerals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Su Song” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
